#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073481 :question/id #uuid "b1d34db3-7f30-5a8c-b233-fd955ac656f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the lines $x - 2y = 2$ and $10y - 5x = -10$ intersect?" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 2; 10y - 5x =-10\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fab4792-0fe1-5a5c-8754-509af469da68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4213192-05bd-5441-ab8f-5601edb4f46a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "intersect at infinitely many points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070315} {:answer/id #uuid "d06783b6-d3ed-5f45-826b-a57ede5f7c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "intersect at one point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070316} {:answer/id #uuid "3a739d2b-bb1e-5a44-b0f7-82240d439868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070317}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2054f218-d7fb-5f4b-bb1e-7462237fd4e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070314}] :question/id #uuid "dffc4e2c-f9ea-5274-92e6-4d3948a4c014" :question/math-academy-id "q-211986" :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 2x - y =-2, l_{2}: 6x - 3y =-12$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b83e75eb-68ab-567d-a460-9470152445c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "intersect at one point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070381} {:answer/id #uuid "fb057ae1-6d19-5e10-9555-8ef14e34ac05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "do not intersect" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070382} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6737ed-0efb-5044-904e-0bbf76ec3016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "intersect at infinitely many points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070383}] :answer-field/id #uuid "19065220-42bb-5118-b439-9be235fa2f10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070380}] :question/id #uuid "c2c1547a-6290-54a8-97be-3db72633dabf" :question/math-academy-id "q-237095" :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = 3x - 4, l_{2}: 12x - 4y = 16$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073482 :question/id #uuid "ab09c3eb-6813-57c6-891b-bf20196cbe93" :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $3x+2y=2$ and $6x+4y=4$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2; 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n{3x + 2y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 6x + 4y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50351666-c691-510f-863c-168c61f9980b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074057 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "3bd07937-83d7-59ef-b1a0-dfa3f718f86f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186070308}] :question/id #uuid "10ef6686-7543-50bc-a43e-d807d37d1e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-208832" :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $x - 4y =-3$ and $2x - 8y =-6$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.The intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec4c6de4-a7fd-5851-accd-9c5156477afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, 2x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071026} {:answer/id #uuid "f81e9c0d-0f7f-5808-ae52-8f03e0039400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071027} {:answer/id #uuid "0e89162b-dfe2-5744-9796-d0f1635ed693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, 3x + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071028} {:answer/id #uuid "49a90275-fbc9-561c-b080-6352f5ee175a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071029} {:answer/id #uuid "677e1be5-b5d0-5254-975c-3550e4fc1853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x, 3x - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071030}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea64686-cfe1-5de1-94d2-5c5e04adbf93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071025}] :question/id #uuid "cb252b96-4a13-53c2-bed5-4b7dd119cf7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-25524" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $9x - 3y =-6$ and $3x - y =-2$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957" :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0731f6-6530-57d1-8101-d583dce27200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238171} {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} {:answer/id #uuid "afd882b4-452c-52f4-9d6a-2ad9a9e823b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239582} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0874aa-3afc-56be-b514-dbaa87daff29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239832} {:answer/id #uuid "d2980861-6b7b-5001-a205-b1b4d310530b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cfb7a5-89a3-5976-92cb-41428186daaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238479} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b7af48-1a47-5f7e-aa94-0ab3a1a923bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fb765b0-7266-558a-8445-d5e7e8dcfb07" :question/math-academy-id "q-105890" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=600.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=2,a=500,$ and ${y}_{0}=600.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-2(y-500),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=400<a=500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3800c8-cb88-5193-ac78-ecad629bac8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800y - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075107} {:answer/id #uuid "cc86f27c-9102-5471-8151-edb2db7a3851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 20y^{2} - 800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075108} {:answer/id #uuid "de0a15f9-0ee2-5d22-9af1-22fcb5891d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075109} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6d6ee-08e6-51ad-b478-11481d79e6d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 + 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075110} {:answer/id #uuid "6537327b-2aa9-5b4f-8d32-77ba1961661c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt} = 800 - 20y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075111}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6cb1533-9217-5c12-ab91-a8596f52e7b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075106}] :question/id #uuid "efe4332d-8d00-5d94-9cfa-87582b5e92ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105893" :question/problem "Given that $y > 40$ and $t > 0$, which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc34311-544b-58ac-b0d4-5ffec4e304fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238574} {:answer/id #uuid "4e20476d-f9bb-5130-8f9e-628e39957f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238728} {:answer/id #uuid "565d5a3d-b07c-5417-aca5-0f14a0b69ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238800} {:answer/id #uuid "90d5ff36-f97d-5e89-b7ec-2b83f4fa6dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=80y(y+20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239310} {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37d7ad7-c270-5174-be15-143df61c1f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2372051-f6b8-5f84-9685-5fee700182de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0fe545c-1008-5ec6-a573-a706bc6b026b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105898" :question/problem "Given that $y>20$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-80(y-20).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=80,$ and $a=20.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>20$ for all $t>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b0d02e3-ea76-54be-9962-5ec9138cf3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5y(y+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241559} {:answer/id #uuid "5eed5d22-c0e1-5129-9594-f6dbaafa7287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5{y}^{2}(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242032} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea7d0ef-a47c-52ec-93ff-921385515791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(9-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242295} {:answer/id #uuid "9fac9354-1235-5fb0-9542-c17bebd4f0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242579} {:answer/id #uuid "e25bbabc-1c66-5c6b-a765-1c2b1cf083e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5({y}^{2}-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fac9354-1235-5fb0-9542-c17bebd4f0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242579} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c886461-a38a-59f3-8cbe-79521a21c395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243492}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23774b05-7a15-54bf-bb21-810326ad31c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106647" :question/problem "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86320bd9-da59-5656-8ab3-124ddf9658b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242355} {:answer/id #uuid "c4580dbb-6ecc-59b2-a1ed-2744eb17551c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176y-22{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242907} {:answer/id #uuid "c50a56a4-5bf6-5a94-ba37-c1be22f8e9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=22{y}^{2}-176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242915} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e66061-8542-5ca6-96ff-02c8087db693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242961} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3e0ae2-02fc-5068-ada2-e9ae87b0b863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176+22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e66061-8542-5ca6-96ff-02c8087db693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242961} :answer-field/id #uuid "e899a014-1dbf-514f-a651-a2c2945018b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5077cc12-6631-56e6-a777-59697d379c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-106651" :question/problem "Given that $y>8$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-22(y-8).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=22,$ and $a=8.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>8$ for all $t>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80dd6caa-b7e5-5430-9255-ebfcfb0f14b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002y(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376016} {:answer/id #uuid "412c8001-8cc2-5d51-ac9a-2a88c39b4385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376017} {:answer/id #uuid "af449a69-bb36-51ff-a07f-9f0eb8312294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.002y(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376018} {:answer/id #uuid "4206d1df-1194-5d9f-bf77-e6035c18064d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376019} {:answer/id #uuid "8071c022-f04c-53d7-b274-41d8c2f12f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412c8001-8cc2-5d51-ac9a-2a88c39b4385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376017} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d187d41-b8f4-5db2-bfe5-4298cd8747f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376015}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e431a13-4453-552d-82ca-1466efb78d08" :question/math-academy-id "q-15591" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited decay problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=3\\,000.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.002,a=1\\,500,$ and ${y}_{0}=3\\,000.$ Notice that ${y}_{0}>a,$ which is required for inhibited decay. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited decay. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-0.002(y-1\\,500),\\,y(0)=1\\,000,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited decay model because ${y}_{0}=1\\,000<a=1\\,500.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "530d441f-02a7-58b9-ab67-967bf0eb2b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=28{y}^{2}-840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397845} {:answer/id #uuid "129b2ea8-1b76-5424-95c9-8017f737962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840y-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397846} {:answer/id #uuid "0659f1ed-771e-5b24-b08f-cc16c97621ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397847} {:answer/id #uuid "84008fcd-ee7c-5c1d-b05c-17d7861ef713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840+28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397848} {:answer/id #uuid "65b83ad8-4ba9-5abe-ac5c-3f8478868400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0659f1ed-771e-5b24-b08f-cc16c97621ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397847} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b25d0f3-fc25-51e5-b7b1-1eea7cc7a67a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397844}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f61f141-3dc0-53a6-8909-845a088a0525" :question/math-academy-id "q-106654" :question/problem "Given that $y>30$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=840-28y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-28(y-30).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=28,$ and $a=30.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>30$ for all $t>0.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a1637b2-831b-5b2c-8230-97f9e2c57121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T^{′}(t) =-kT(T - 25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075119} {:answer/id #uuid "c48530ee-0dd0-5b69-989e-1519b77c44cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T^{′}(t) = k(T - 25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075120} {:answer/id #uuid "07c2f09c-1d36-5b65-ab08-7c1af98a0a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T^{′}(t) = kT(T - 25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075121} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0209ec-2c1f-5140-b7f5-c80513586152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T^{′}(t) =-k(T - 25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075122} {:answer/id #uuid "65ae7d88-d4f4-5879-9ad4-a09c82443506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T^{′}(t) = kT(T + 25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075123}] :answer-field/id #uuid "385b574a-86cb-5cbc-a02b-25339e6092c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075118}] :question/id #uuid "246dbe47-9137-5b96-af87-04ddded3bed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105917" :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t > 0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is $25^{∘}C$, which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342581f4-cabc-5059-a251-2692ca6f27ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321542} {:answer/id #uuid "71be98e8-1e44-50bb-9bb9-b32dd2edc859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322040} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e6a8e3-742a-59bc-850f-65fe5a3e9e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322796} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e99ed-fb8f-592b-af00-7b9afa2790e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322949} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b01d91-18e1-599b-8abf-64d7291ac4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323328}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b80c3e-e59a-547b-a782-41612e4da31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-67(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241227} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccf8a1b-29f1-5b0a-80ad-9958a78a6231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-145(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241422} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5e0f28-4de0-5f1e-ae81-d2cf901b0128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241889} {:answer/id #uuid "c6be0d3b-ed68-5a2f-af1a-64dc02f7559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} {:answer/id #uuid "e362934b-110b-510b-8990-c6bccbecf578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-145)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6be0d3b-ed68-5a2f-af1a-64dc02f7559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242933} :answer-field/id #uuid "93430b60-75e1-51bd-a136-498bba75fcbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243715}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ba4e3cf-6728-56eb-8731-1748c4ffae5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106883" :question/problem "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad9d8a6-7aba-56a6-afbc-91568d96c3cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-76k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241412} {:answer/id #uuid "2b82751d-3119-5dfe-92bf-dd1ff296563d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-200)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241566} {:answer/id #uuid "d98d1bb8-6a1f-57a1-837b-46446db882ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243117} {:answer/id #uuid "db649853-ab75-536f-99a1-1fb71fae0e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-200(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243128} {:answer/id #uuid "e321b854-ed39-5316-9cb9-9d7725a4859a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=200k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d98d1bb8-6a1f-57a1-837b-46446db882ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243117} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9c0873-1b25-595b-825b-fb1a95161c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d9f1ee5-0c1f-52df-9e3a-c2e55b83891f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106934" :question/problem "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b5f534-9e32-5a0a-b2bc-2ba8e2c86a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242660} {:answer/id #uuid "b384362d-2277-54cd-9282-ce1d30347703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242745} {:answer/id #uuid "c6193ec8-264d-5ffa-8793-75573063af90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-90k(T-19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242925} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d698b-8905-5477-8ba9