ortional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ec0e10-da72-5c68-8c2a-a1567403fea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-75k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241378} {:answer/id #uuid "89da823e-61ae-5588-8b2b-ecce0fbfcaee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-450k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242393} {:answer/id #uuid "b581ec27-9282-5462-83a5-db99c3def9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242765} {:answer/id #uuid "b96a94b4-0759-595d-b5e9-8c08089e01e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} {:answer/id #uuid "d243e252-a064-5c78-96c1-fe946bd46182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96a94b4-0759-595d-b5e9-8c08089e01e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242806} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ed1cc7-4ade-5c7d-a721-5e4187d31c3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cd430a4-4af3-5fcc-b21d-c7663e55325d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106937" :question/problem "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f5f9e59-3c36-5807-b8f1-60d5c867aee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef51c8-be4a-5a5f-b4b6-7b12be0bb5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399583} {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} {:answer/id #uuid "49678131-628e-553a-9844-5179f76e8b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399585} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d4bea4-992f-5fb1-be9b-5bc63898ddea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7c9128-4613-5828-914f-598e6ef01189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d44d9a6b-035e-5168-a3f6-72ab2e21a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106878" :question/problem "The rate at which a body cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the body in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273200} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9183c-594a-50ff-89ee-e5298cd3be92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273490} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc2c9b-f809-527a-9228-06e37aeb1131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc440f-ad27-570f-b514-432bec694cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15396bb-b0f6-5ba4-9f17-f68ca77b61da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1495237b-afbf-5205-a022-9f6acc5cbda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272570} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8e38af-c5d0-507f-a1bd-aae08b27500d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273439} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f377d8-6bb6-5509-84d5-efce6120f034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273947} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4ed67-f97a-54dc-81d8-37f939ad8898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd7cc63-77c2-5834-8ea5-22b666b6543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274316} :answer-field/id #uuid "217e660e-5140-5d1b-956d-f278238bb223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15595" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} {:answer/id #uuid "45e54fee-1ace-542a-8f41-af48ff15ba39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc5e0-ba4e-56c2-8bd8-2c6ee24e4cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242007} {:answer/id #uuid "a245084d-1bb7-5412-bc76-5d4162c98715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242601} {:answer/id #uuid "d36cea6e-e345-5856-9738-8be126828c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40e0dff5-bfb1-5212-94c6-b1dadc022a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241760} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa52215b-9a2a-5e79-b263-c1fe67ce6a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b60de53-dea5-5dd2-908e-421b529445e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241219} {:answer/id #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241380} {:answer/id #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242096} {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} :answer-field/id #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123a42f8-2a35-5617-ba7d-f1b4d2c746b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241335} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc08283-0067-51f0-81a0-558fa4cdcf8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242008} {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} {:answer/id #uuid "9c820d27-170e-5c1f-81bb-f4a2542fecd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242546} {:answer/id #uuid "ad515dcc-0231-5912-ac0e-df7d88012a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8346bdeb-1005-53c8-8841-f50949f617dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242327} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbd68ea-f66a-5917-98d1-898732b44fd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39da46ca-e83e-580d-8039-34194e24ef5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ae70af-887b-5770-9ba5-23db38cd4801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241332} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} {:answer/id #uuid "4231eca7-f91a-5287-acdd-0be0665d65cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241773} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d27d97-2c83-55f0-970f-d85dcbf228a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242597} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d7b5b-1863-5704-89a4-3e2c90453ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cbae164-802b-58ab-91b2-3faf9544773d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241579} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec7d5808-b789-5047-9d21-ccfd0fbce57b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ff863d3-8088-512b-858b-b0b33302607f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "893f4780-2c8b-522b-8087-2fc24822ebd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395764} {:answer/id #uuid "93cd5cdd-b047-544f-b7e7-fee685cf62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} {:answer/id #uuid "ef24ffa0-3d6e-5335-9009-9906ff059c99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395766} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f97ca-3e74-5b74-90a6-ef1513732aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395767} {:answer/id #uuid "96517c6b-bae9-503f-9f98-d3f004b861a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93cd5cdd-b047-544f-b7e7-fee685cf62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395765} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca47f9c-32af-5d7e-92a5-e9a1ab61bc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395763}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02ce9872-8986-5e67-bea1-b49d589c24ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-15589" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12647afe-fb4f-5f2b-9326-19bf07c40a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397886} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd2a414-b8e6-52ba-9a39-7d6bbb93cce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397887} {:answer/id #uuid "6612145e-ab64-58c5-8ac9-574cb5ec22a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397888} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e45ac-2ae6-5e93-b31c-67cedb5a0fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59f961-3401-5e50-8b21-92d3facaf654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\cos{t}, \\quad y=\\sin{t}, \\qquad  0\\leq t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$\n\n![](images/2e/2ec95dd061fa6b7f09c9f899a1f6483b4ea7d5127372ddd6eefb74d6ed10c14c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -\\sin{t}, \\qquad y'(t)=\\cos{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\cos t}{-\\sin t} = -\\cot t.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021b2e51-7b32-5d61-854c-fc1a9ef58def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333007} {:answer/id #uuid "28e9726e-00db-5f34-87d4-9a487ff7e73f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333367} {:answer/id #uuid "580f0635-8c83-5156-8568-562dba7b5e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333793} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7d169b-2f30-53ff-b665-0cd112f09889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334109} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a39baa-5e77-52ef-9085-55292ea23958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021b2e51-7b32-5d61-854c-fc1a9ef58def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333007} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1d36e2-933d-5160-bd71-41a68070fc12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e23a09c-558b-5f22-8055-2a530503094c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6731" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9090bad5-bdc1-57ce-827a-037fa0e55484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cdot {e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368636} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad2b68d-5bdb-5b61-8dff-cbfbe61dd5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cdot {e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368637} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd1dcda-4ea5-5915-b0b1-9f263ce60838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\cdot {e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368638} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1120e6-0b07-5e9b-9743-a1f6a530953a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368639} {:answer/id #uuid "ece13b9b-8337-58e5-b3c1-7806c09c8d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1120e6-0b07-5e9b-9743-a1f6a530953a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{{e}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368639} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ffb1e1-b6d4-5298-80bc-bfbfec0cbcd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368635}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a00aae7-ab0b-5d69-8218-d506362c55ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-6809" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4t}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=5$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4\\cdot 5}{{e}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{{e}^{5}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426f5205-e4dc-5e33-b797-1f55019e14d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288483} {:answer/id #uuid "988141a9-9be5-59b2-8abd-837c9c5652aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289054} {:answer/id #uuid "cd825353-57a8-52b4-9d0b-ae4303b47a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289403} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e9d989-c2e7-5e61-85c6-2c964c681758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289670} {:answer/id #uuid "f9628a6b-8330-5dfc-b634-fd26bd7aa9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "988141a9-9be5-59b2-8abd-837c9c5652aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289054} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b257f8-6e6b-5962-b0e6-3ea368c98ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f7deccd-b00e-58b4-a06a-44a78bd3e26d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20988" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b77bdde611689095cdea6a3a08ce9828b9e20040283dc0c251250538a5cfba3.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{6t-2}=\\frac{t}{3t-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9dd0123-b8e1-55f5-8f39-55cc94febb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9dd0123-b8e1-55f5-8f39-55cc94febb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319902} :answer-field/id #uuid "079d2083-b887-5746-8f4a-2763a37f8c60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48e499b2-6d1b-5fe4-a033-41b4b4365949" :question/math-academy-id "q-353340" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b1dc90950ba127e90fce400bc7ed1b6e8dd8e710ff880ab5949521b02a258a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=4{e}^{-t},\\,y={t}^{2}+6t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+6}{(-4{e}^{-t})}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)+6}{(-4{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8e}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0f10ff0-18c6-5914-a313-73bb4cb93ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319929} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2d3244-6f8c-503e-84d8-0399c320617e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320390}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d193d39c-471b-5fec-8b86-474bbc1fb6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-353341" :question/problem "![](images/18/1817c65aea37112d93f2935801a0e7dcd654706a72f729810272f48e95d39665.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /6}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321112 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57e66287-df20-5352-8c57-82613f6d9872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57e66287-df20-5352-8c57-82613f6d9872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319651} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d2aa81-56a9-5ec0-8780-da583c96885a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320314}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0af90cd-0dda-500f-9fc6-6a569abe78d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353343" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t}{2{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3t}{{e}^{2t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2}{{e}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77052cc4-8eea-5cef-8b05-26c9c4eef0f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77052cc4-8eea-5cef-8b05-26c9c4eef0f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319748} :answer-field/id #uuid "85e642b6-7d86-530e-bd66-8c4ef03c3bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c960a70d-cb11-51ca-88d4-50c4fa7900ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-353344" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=5t-3,\\,y={t}^{4}+2t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4{t}^{3}+2}{5}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}+2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab36b16-5bde-52a2-af10-016c5c78e4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320145} :answer-field/id #uuid "3297beab-a596-5653-8a3c-f59721811ee1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1a4b6d5-bbe2-5c0f-8f42-413948326feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-353346" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=8t-3,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=4.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}=16\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =16\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =16(2) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c3b133-15d8-5457-8b33-856a0f15034b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7c3b133-15d8-5457-8b33-856a0f15034b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319891} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee9d754-1e3f-5127-b952-ef485c0de56c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7ab1fc4-f7f2-55b9-a74c-b824a475ac09" :question/math-academy-id "q-353347" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t},\\,y=2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{3{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cacfdc-f4c4-5be9-b447-d4c9efbbfc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333170} {:answer/id #uuid "34681187-f695-5613-9d30-15855a18bbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333470} {:answer/id #uuid "4b60efb3-7267-5c64-b29b-e068b4bd8d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333680} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b3b5b6-c452-5e07-a57d-d43b942dc787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334598} {:answer/id #uuid "da8875f4-cacd-5ead-a1ea-d5ee3514b0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b60efb3-7267-5c64-b29b-e068b4bd8d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333680} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db64bab-5920-507a-a297-6b50b824c9f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335293}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "812a8e96-41b1-529e-a7f2-fc31fe645a3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6810" :question/problem "![](images/49/4969bf3cea8290c76e79419ed0160a8c1f92d7415250305df56eb8128ad15cf4.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}+t-1,\\,y={t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}+1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{4{t}^{3}+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(-4)+1} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5acc6cd8-f001-5394-a6fb-c1dd0fc5b3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333819} {:answer/id #uuid "911bd715-c47b-52fc-8b17-1d94b494a8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334283} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a0b785-553c-5b96-8cde-3a695db73d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334450} {:answer/id #uuid "aef4e7d5-b6bf-5eef-a5a0-2697f1745f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334529} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b6f7ec-a4b3-53d6-a622-e814425b78ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef4e7d5-b6bf-5eef-a5a0-2697f1745f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334529} :answer-field/id #uuid "b356f94f-948d-5358-9ef9-3fb6cfeb2cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335566}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18b27deb-1f86-5103-a5ac-bd0f135dadc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-6816" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=({t}^{2}-1){e}^{-t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}}{2{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{1}{2}(1+2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2(1)-(1)^{2}){e}^{-3(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2-1){e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5b0db0-5ba2-5adf-9792-474f45d828e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333533} {:answer/id #uuid "8d805541-01a4-5d0f-86bc-885f7c5ffeeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334252} {:answer/id #uuid "a32b15fa-90c9-5ba2-98ea-24ef327f9b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334422} {:answer/id #uuid "c0435c64-9140-52bd-be23-669676cba748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334656} {:answer/id #uuid "e2903a75-bc2d-543d-98eb-50836c3e192b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2903a75-bc2d-543d-98eb-50836c3e192b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334961} :answer-field/id #uuid "449e742b-3005-5dcd-8a3f-1a65f1ef7162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335368}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43a0f51e-6281-52c0-b715-729aa4f5b61d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6848" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d4abbff66a225f92a8e4f5d30fb86b0b8e205b8a047ed3ed9d5af7290ac89c6.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{5}-2t,\\,y=1-{t}^{3},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-3{t}^{2}}{5{t}^{4}-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3(-1)^{2}}{5(-1)^{4}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e92365-398c-55c8-b9d2-5949b0615644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e92365-398c-55c8-b9d2-5949b0615644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368469} :answer-field/id #uuid "2537c273-fce3-516f-a3c3-e43d012cdc8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e71f7895-9560-5f17-b152-99d6399eac50" :question/math-academy-id "q-353349" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,y=3{t}^{3/2},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{t})}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}=9t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fd7882-e12b-5d1e-bda2-674211a5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395778} {:answer/id #uuid "61e0f80f-69b1-578c-857a-82bc0682835a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395779} {:answer/id #uuid "26a77c96-4bef-5e82-8889-1c4a9ce7d523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395780} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c50146-0d7c-501d-bf37-4d8d838c3cef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b090b-3cbc-511c-95ab-35fc8c1fc057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395781} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d7a736-a513-51fb-b585-d99caa2d0531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395777}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b1f64f-9248-57a2-a310-050eb4c80ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-20849" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9371d5e2bb921b4f5e58423bbce492f2b555122ee42bd861040b57d01070353.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-1\\le t<1,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8t}{2}=4t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =4(0) \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401845} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401841}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "886ea0dd-2f3b-5230-a912-ca494bbb228c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401855} {:answer/id #uuid "1b58ab2c-a7ae-5296-9ea9-aaaf06b0d86c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401856} {:answer/id #uuid "3480faea-4f30-5410-8c55-291a77967982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401857} {:answer/id #uuid "1933a3b0-751e-5adc-a9a1-355a8352674b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401858} {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9dd7c11-42ae-55e1-8e09-7e1133cc18c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401859} :answer-field/id #uuid "532bfa22-570e-5521-a58b-09bf6df9181b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c14e172-0d42-58ef-86b2-05fb93de9caa" :question/math-academy-id "q-20990" :question/problem "![](images/58/5855e92b04ffbf8447d32c7edb190cd16046a13e8808e6ffaade1f59e414c869.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=2t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+2t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+2}{2{e}^{t}(t+1)}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b7074a-75b7-5dfa-ba96-9067b0b0c2da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403274} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca59391-4b16-5de2-b31a-e434042882de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403275} {:answer/id #uuid "b7da2d97-ee19-5b91-aae1-d3e1ef8e41cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403276} {:answer/id #uuid "c0595185-9dbe-5e61-833d-f322b3bc5d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403277} {:answer/id #uuid "efa241d0-aa74-5ee0-84e5-879e44d2f438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0595185-9dbe-5e61-833d-f322b3bc5d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403277} :answer-field/id #uuid "cade3433-2a7c-573e-ae14-9f4f0ad1cbde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403273}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef78af33-68fa-5c96-9f7c-05772b13cffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-6812" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=({t}^{3}-1){e}^{t},\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{({t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-1){e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{({0}^{3}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-1){e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{(-1)\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc882f2-3f87-5d1b-873e-989959c29c45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403281} {:answer/id #uuid "571f5f41-efb0-58e5-a46f-8c213ca0a973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403282} {:answer/id #uuid "73cec9e6-12bd-5c23-9094-78dadbc4e7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403283} {:answer/id #uuid "a99bf228-d02b-5fa9-b6b7-158b7cd89312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403284} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb8bf5d-1f44-5559-a2f8-15d082ac4b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a99bf228-d02b-5fa9-b6b7-158b7cd89312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403284} :answer-field/id #uuid "81664d19-fcb9-599f-8db6-ec3e35d93bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403280}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b50faac3-57b3-5534-a1d3-8f76087cb51c" :question/math-academy-id "q-6836" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04488a59-6ffe-5356-ac3f-e74eeb54d1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288061} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8997e3-1fe3-58c6-8171-ece7ebae387b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288312} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed485ea-7355-5812-b046-45c3a1d3e1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{4{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288892} {:answer/id #uuid "d60a4cd8-4221-5930-822d-5407abdc3f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289452} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f5de57-f5aa-5c96-938a-b8c5217f9660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f5de57-f5aa-5c96-938a-b8c5217f9660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289665} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7251d58-9b9c-5089-ac38-6e385106aa2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290193}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f252b1a9-57b3-57ad-97f5-a61e75b56356" :question/math-academy-id "q-20991" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}}{4{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-4{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=5,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}(5-1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16dfb55b-8af4-596f-a315-353765522560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333194} {:answer/id #uuid "c16a839e-1b3e-54f0-9222-740ef19de000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334665} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d5d8b0-70e4-5f14-b80f-590b782f7d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334729} {:answer/id #uuid "ce913bb5-7127-5bc9-acac-45bf128689d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334790} {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e0065-d183-5d46-90dd-110f8b159fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73a45f9-70e6-5c98-9255-49149400966d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6808" :question/problem "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8ce46e-daf7-55c0-89df-337038a09ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236422} {:answer/id #uuid "68ed4719-9dea-5337-9667-8f3e5657f5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236480} {:answer/id #uuid "7f133e8a-78f1-5235-b7a3-a3e3ba78ba25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236604} {:answer/id #uuid "d944ae68-297d-5663-a79f-21d577d3bda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a55434-2f0c-5beb-95aa-d1250e59d512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "962d8665-19cf-5669-bc0c-46b711cf96ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-102922" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0854b8df-7a7a-57f6-b376-2a218e36ff2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235854} {:answer/id #uuid "6acdc17f-072f-5f68-86af-7572c7fd6f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236490} {:answer/id #uuid "8afcd281-1318-594a-a9ba-52f65fa8ef56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236686} {:answer/id #uuid "bf44f6a5-6c3e-5582-ba6b-2ec5a73ba371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237032} {:answer/id #uuid "e72bd93b-5cde-5f30-8245-c6eb24cfb3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf44f6a5-6c3e-5582-ba6b-2ec5a73ba371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237032} :answer-field/id #uuid "067447ca-a94b-5969-ab12-e75c926f9806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237450}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f469ae9f-d511-5b2e-b179-c38dadb0919b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102940" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,y=3{t}^{5}+{t}^{3},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}(5{t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}(5{t}^{2}+1)}{4t}=\\frac{3t(5{t}^{2}+1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(1)(5(1)^{2}+1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} {:answer/id #uuid "4a01b9e0-c4f0-5704-a712-09144764bfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236292} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2e0013-e151-51e9-ad4a-bbb0f6be0b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237009} {:answer/id #uuid "c954ba9f-6430-5221-9f47-5bf333b81728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237094} {:answer/id #uuid "f813ee90-74dd-524e-9db4-b206cc8dc2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a48016-4413-5cfa-822b-c23d4158c7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7720b821-7c21-51f2-9679-46c0e6df53a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102941" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{4}{\\pi }{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{8}{\\pi }t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{\\pi }t)}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fb0b4b-0bf1-5c76-8696-c067c4ad8969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272604} {:answer/id #uuid "6347253c-9fd4-5926-94fe-94155ab5318b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273242} {:answer/id #uuid "722f7007-9e15-5b79-bcc7-366580ece601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273367} {:answer/id #uuid "880d64e6-f8cb-55ff-8d8e-f7e554ac9ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273554} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c124b-193b-5381-9981-884d4b7bd866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "722f7007-9e15-5b79-bcc7-366580ece601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273367} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e582ec-54ef-57eb-ade9-5e1ede765e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d08529b-8573-5553-9a9b-ff64d66c71ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-15457" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y={cos}^{2}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346e81fc-49e8-5206-8118-642a76aff3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272854} {:answer/id #uuid "848dbf29-ba73-58ac-a73a-39bdd77f8bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{8e}{27}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273525} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca28c54-4cd9-59d9-8637-13eeddcf1cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{9e}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273601} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf5f8f4-d801-50ab-a06f-979e93d31278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273740} {:answer/id #uuid "a64af6bb-9a4f-5bcd-ac79-4944cc823d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{8e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346e81fc-49e8-5206-8118-642a76aff3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272854} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a335fd5-65a0-5d78-ae55-0404e4850f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2399836b-91ff-571b-9534-908c753b0355" :question/math-academy-id "q-15475" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{1/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}},\\,1<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}{e}^{-1/t}}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}{e}^{-1/2}}{({2}^{2}-1)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{3}^{3/2}\\sqrt{e}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6edb1b-f7b7-5718-8cda-0dd5a71a50be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272774} {:answer/id #uuid "2d253935-f522-5f06-9ee4-61b8bb0a1972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272789} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13d3fc-0795-5608-98ad-09cff47ca45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272799} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2e7d1f-a9f1-5d66-a415-8d8c4115d124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273303} {:answer/id #uuid "de45196d-d0c1-55a1-8019-f950885e3562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6edb1b-f7b7-5718-8cda-0dd5a71a50be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272774} :answer-field/id #uuid "f570cb94-97be-59e0-a398-fb3eb82d9321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275067}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18a41cc2-6786-5c7c-b54f-7a6c8dbb881f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15476" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})},\\,y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({e}^{t}),\\,-\\infty <t<1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}, \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}⁡({e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}⁡({e}^{t})}{(\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}{{sin}^{2}⁡({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}⁡({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3/2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e83485-026d-51c3-a54b-33ed9e867b92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a64ef463-d187-5f34-bc23-8e25a68dd5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-348303" :question/problem "![](images/59/59f053b9a122018d78cd9c30f5a53cd67cd2ff645d02ccb6d311e6183e081102.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644} :answer-field/id #uuid "88549d85-6690-5559-9bdd-e3b23c490bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320444}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d2c31a-1162-5af9-962c-a704ed1fd8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-353350" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568} :answer-field/id #uuid "6920c4ef-fcdf-5331-9ff1-0d0ddf66b7c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6474a49e-c58a-508a-9e50-a787079f364a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353351" :question/problem "![](images/35/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352} :answer-field/id #uuid "abecd077-f709-5b82-9c40-6c4dc262a11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae92af54-aa04-5ea8-bfcd-575703012570" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e634f729-6fad-5eee-8c7f-dd8fad161e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e634f729-6fad-5eee-8c7f-dd8fad161e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368396} :answer-field/id #uuid "01554cb6-b048-5d22-9b7e-6a54c35e86d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fd0997f-50c1-5e89-b401-28e2788c518b" :question/math-academy-id "q-348305" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t}}{9{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-6{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=4,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}(4-1)}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}\\cdot 3}{2} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ee5fc9-0606-5e80-bb6e-fc3422758a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72ee5fc9-0606-5e80-bb6e-fc3422758a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368472} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c6cf65-f4d1-540f-8883-933b5cb9cf59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "282535b6-7f0b-5681-8ed7-3e9b8413bd06" :question/math-academy-id "q-353363" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e807c1b4-ec12-578e-adfe-38f82bc92701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368630} {:answer/id #uuid "12e80468-7314-5375-a06c-ac6bbe01fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368631} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4fa272-e3a0-5a72-966e-a6949699eb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368632} {:answer/id #uuid "bbab7461-db2d-5d59-b338-baad2f7e5541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368633} {:answer/id #uuid "584d0dc4-bafb-53d6-8b06-244aa54043ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12e80468-7314-5375-a06c-ac6bbe01fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368631} :answer-field/id #uuid "6802ba7f-a62f-596f-82ab-3508a6bdc4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c3b2996-4d45-5fac-b139-960f69bf9ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-6730" :question/problem "![](images/94/9434dda63c08fc1202d16ab5410fe7061050515876ffa473d8720db39079af3e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60199c4-dc25-5568-af40-de39cd26589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401071} {:answer/id #uuid "41c812bd-cb3b-5fc5-aef0-fb585602e943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401072} {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc02f7-9666-528e-9191-ebdcde2135fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401074} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea8ed64-c406-5164-b57d-847952b47323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc7c1c-c03b-5382-bd6b-5a9beec491d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9e0331-ffe6-5489-b436-faf1f0bea590" :question/math-academy-id "q-15469" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}+1,\\,y={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4t({t}^{2}+1)}{6{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({t}^{2}+1)}{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2((1)^{2}+1)}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad14a3-f4a1-50de-ae0b-9d72a2b1d32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433350} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9da3b51-53f9-58fc-b00c-db0ea43214f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433351}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62968591-e72a-5306-bd3f-0e762f4b0368" :question/math-academy-id "q-353352" :question/problem "![](images/03/0353d9a2b8c8e46552e0816658628f67b58c0c6bef51bd05261becac42a55798.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2),\\,-2<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t+2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{t+2})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{0}(0+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1)(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6a1fb9-a35a-5b64-913e-c9df98bccbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{24}{11}\\,x+\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333430} {:answer/id #uuid "53eed6ee-9b5a-505e-be78-f6bb4c4f4050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333753} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} {:answer/id #uuid "95bef049-706d-5788-8e10-cb68b22507f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334321} {:answer/id #uuid "a6894270-0960-537c-a6f0-979b8b3809d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac34767-b922-5f65-a668-1c1e1e6c8f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335447}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94e72be-f23a-535e-b719-cf664553611b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6857" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f56a41ed-59a1-5429-9c88-30a68cc3b4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{3}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082410} {:answer/id #uuid "0a46d561-b2f3-58d4-94a4-01099db3bb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082411} {:answer/id #uuid "1068a7ae-c66e-5103-a69c-cd043a388acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082412} {:answer/id #uuid "383e37b1-7785-5316-993d-06f2ecc4bf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082413} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3d43fe-583d-5c3c-b161-e97547fb6627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 = \\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082414}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dce6874e-77a5-5de0-abb5-e4403a900937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082409}] :question/id #uuid "c6d49020-9a4a-5e6f-8526-58e27d438cd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-6862" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{2} - 2t, y = t^{5}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t =-1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2483de-bb9b-57f7-a79f-34885e11205d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253803} {:answer/id #uuid "c65aceae-e84b-5651-9ab5-ee308d3662ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb258778-9d8d-556c-8e5b-b5614a8787a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255476} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0c7cf4-bb80-57c1-a9c1-3db0dee8005d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d4061c-5bca-5874-a889-0e5b0bcb0553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "405e30d2-1994-59ac-94e7-5e7998f82913" :question/math-academy-id "q-122698" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d7761-9781-53c7-9bbc-78084d322e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{9},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272450} {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a5947-d436-56d5-880b-0465859f1964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{7},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273188} {:answer/id #uuid "80184777-1859-51fd-907f-4bb3c159a4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273486} {:answer/id #uuid "e955007e-17f7-5db4-9fbf-cecc47ec6559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} :answer-field/id #uuid "affbb4e4-fcfc-5f64-b6b5-e473fd4f7c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51cd744a-a0db-52ff-b937-07f4a84310d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15564" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287acad-245f-58bd-be4f-6dbce3ede11e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318564}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9181afbc-9550-51c6-b28c-f82450210472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348109" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbbd214-a055-53f2-b3b5-67bd9aed6a47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee152229-0df5-5943-83fa-bcee1ecff76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a109daf-d742-5b15-87da-27961e643b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}]}
